    "body": "Fixes #168\n\n## Problem\n\nA `*` followed by text that is never closed, like `'[\\/]$,*,\\.bak$,...` in the `g:netrw_sort_sequence` docs, is parsed as a `tag` with a MISSING closing `*`, so Nvim conceals the `*`.\n\n## Solution\n\nAdd a \"NOT tag\" case to `_word_common`: a `*` immediately followed by text is plain text unless the closing `*` follows, in the same style as the existing \"NOT optionlink\" cases. The text uses the same token as the tag text, so real tags parse exactly as before.\n\n- New corpus test \"NOT a tag: `*` without a closing `*`\" with the line from the issue. It fails on master.\n- In \"NOT a tag\" and \"NOT optionlink #7 #14\", the `ERROR` nodes around stray `*`s are gone.\n- I parsed all 138 files in Nvim's `runtime/doc` before and after. No file gets a new error, `map.txt` no longer has one, and the number of `tag` nodes is unchanged (10943). `autocmd.txt` and `channel.txt` still fail in the CI corpus step, same as on master.\n\n`src/` is regenerated with tree-sitter 0.26.3, which reproduces master's `parser.c` exactly.\n",
    "closed_at": null,
